graph growth-guide {
  meta: { description: "Growth guide prompt", type: "prompt", category: "ai", author: "AITOS Team" }

  let prompt = set(key: "_promptGrowthGuide", value: ">>> CONTEXT: This section is ONLY relevant when the user EXPLICITLY asks you to create, modify, or debug a graph/growth. For normal conversations, SKIP this section entirely. <<<\n\n## Creating Growth (后生图)\n\n### Growth Types\n\n1. **tool** - Background functionality (no UI)\n   - Simple: just logic graph\n   \n2. **app** - Interactive UI\n   - Complex: needs UI graph + handle-click graph\n\n### CRITICAL: Input/Output Declarations\n\nGraphs declare their entry and exit points in the data structure itself using `in:` and `out:`:\n\n- `in:` — what parameters this graph expects (name and type)\n- `out:` — which node's value IS the graph's return value\n\n**AI reads the data structure, not the execution order.** The graph's output is an explicit data field (`out:`), not an implicit \"last statement\" convention.\n\nExample:\n```\ngraph weather-check {\n  meta: { description: \"Check weather\", type: \"tool\", icon: \"🌤\" }\n  in: location: string\n  out: data\n\n  let data = httpGet(url: \"https://api.weather.com/\\{{location}}\")\n  let save = set(key: \"weatherData\", value: \"\\{{data}}\")\n}\n```\n\nThe graph returns the value of node `data` (the httpGet result), not `save`. When another graph calls this via `execFile(file: \"weather-check\")`, it gets the weather data directly.\n\nWhen you call `list-growths()`, you see each graph's declared inputs and output — no need to read source just to discover parameters.\n\n**Always declare `out:` for tool-type graphs.** Without it, the runtime falls back to the last node's value, which is error-prone.\n\nSave it with one call:\n```\nsave-growth(name: \"weather-check\", acs: \"<the ACS code above>\")\n```\n\n### App-Type Requirements\n\nApp-type growths need TWO graphs:\n1. Main graph: renders UI\n2. handle-click graph: handles user interactions\n\n### CRITICAL: Graph Decomposition Strategy\n\n**NEVER write a single graph with many branches.** Each graph should have ONE clear responsibility.\n\nFor complex apps, decompose into multiple subgraphs:\n\n**BAD - One giant graph with 15 branches:**\n```\ngraph handle-click {\n  // 15 if-branches for 15 buttons... too complex!\n}\n```\n\n**GOOD - Decompose by responsibility:**\n```\ngrowths/my-app/\n  ├── handle-click.acs        # Router: dispatches to sub-handlers\n  ├── handle-digit.acs        # Handles digit buttons\n  ├── handle-operator.acs     # Handles operator buttons\n  ├── handle-equals.acs       # Handles equals button\n  └── handle-clear.acs        # Handles clear button\n```\n\nThe router pattern:\n```\ngraph handle-click {\n  let targetId = get(key: \"clickTargetId\")\n  let isDigit = startsWith(text: \"\\{{targetId}}\", prefix: \"btn-digit\")\n  let isOp = startsWith(text: \"\\{{targetId}}\", prefix: \"btn-op\")\n  let isEquals = eq(a: \"\\{{targetId}}\", b: \"btn-equals\")\n  let isClear = eq(a: \"\\{{targetId}}\", b: \"btn-clear\")\n  let routeDigit = if isDigit {\n    let exec = execFile(file: \"my-app/handle-digit\")\n  }\n  let routeOp = if isOp {\n    let exec = execFile(file: \"my-app/handle-operator\")\n  }\n  let routeEquals = if isEquals {\n    let exec = execFile(file: \"my-app/handle-equals\")\n  }\n  let routeClear = if isClear {\n    let exec = execFile(file: \"my-app/handle-clear\")\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### CRITICAL: Pattern Matching for Groups\n\nWhen handling groups of similar buttons (digits, operators, etc.), use `startsWith` + `slice` pattern:\n\n**BAD - Separate check for each digit:**\n```\nlet is0 = eq(a: \"\\{{targetId}}\", b: \"btn-0\")\nlet is1 = eq(a: \"\\{{targetId}}\", b: \"btn-1\")\nlet is2 = eq(a: \"\\{{targetId}}\", b: \"btn-2\")\n// ... 7 more\n```\n\n**GOOD - Use startsWith + slice:**\n```\nlet isDigit = startsWith(text: \"\\{{targetId}}\", prefix: \"btn-digit-\")\nlet routeDigit = if isDigit {\n  let exec = execFile(file: \"my-app/handle-digit\")\n}\n```\n\nThen in `handle-digit.acs`, extract the digit:\n```\ngraph handle-digit {\n  let targetId = get(key: \"clickTargetId\")\n  let digit = slice(array: \"\\{{targetId}}\", start: 10)\n  // \"btn-digit-7\" -> \"7\"\n  let current = get(key: \"calcDisplay\")\n  // ... use \\{{digit}} in calculation\n}\n```\n\n### CRITICAL: How to Save Graphs\n\n**Use save-growth() to save each graph.** One call does everything: compile, validate, register, persist, index, and log.\n\nFor a simple tool:\n```\nsave-growth(name: \"weather-check\", acs: \"graph weather-check { ... }\")\n```\n\nFor an app with subgraphs, save each graph separately:\n```\nsave-growth(name: \"calculator\", acs: \"graph calculator { ... }\")\nsave-growth(name: \"calculator/handle-click\", acs: \"graph handle-click { ... }\")\nsave-growth(name: \"calculator/handle-digit\", acs: \"graph handle-digit { ... }\")\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- ALWAYS use save-growth() - NEVER use set(key: \"__graph_xxx\") or writeLocal for ACS code\n- Subgraph names use slash: \"calculator/handle-digit\"\n- Type, icon, and description are auto-read from the graph's `meta` block - no need to pass them explicitly\n- If save-growth returns { success: false, error: \"...\" }, fix the error and retry\n\n### CRITICAL: Reference Syntax in ACS\n\n- Use `\\{{nodeId}}` to reference another node's result within the SAME graph\n- These references are resolved at RUNTIME when the graph executes\n- Do NOT try to escape `\\{{}}` with backslash - just write them normally\n- save-growth reads ACS source directly, so `\\{{}}` in your code will be preserved\n\n### CRITICAL: Diagnosing and Fixing Existing Growths\n\nWhen a growth has bugs or performance issues, use this workflow:\n\n1. **Detect issues** - Call `analyze-telemetry()` to get error hotspots, error rates, and performance metrics from historical data\n2. **Inspect code** - Call `get-growth-code(name: \"xxx\")` to view the source of a problematic growth\n3. **Check creation history** - Call `get-growth-log(name: \"xxx\")` to see past creation attempts and errors\n4. **Fix the code** - Write corrected ACS code and call `save-growth(name: \"xxx\", acs: \"...\")` to overwrite the existing growth\n5. **Verify** - Call `analyze-telemetry()` again to confirm errors are resolved\n\nExample workflow:\n```\n// Step 1: Find problematic growths\nanalyze-telemetry()\n\n// Step 2: Inspect a growth with high error rate\nget-growth-code(name: \"calculator/handle-click\")\n\n// Step 3: Check its creation history\nget-growth-log(name: \"calculator/handle-click\")\n\n// Step 4: Fix and overwrite\nsave-growth(name: \"calculator/handle-click\", acs: \"graph handle-click { ... }\")\n\n// Step 5: Verify the fix\nanalyze-telemetry()\n```\n\n**Rules:**\n- Use `save-growth()` with the SAME name to overwrite an existing growth - this updates the code in-place\n- Always check `get-growth-log()` before creating a new growth to learn from past mistakes\n- Use `list-growths()` to see all available growths\n\n### if Subgraph Scope\n\nNodes inside `if`/`else`/`loop`/`forEach` subgraphs can reference nodes from the parent scope:\n```\nlet targetId = get(key: \"clickTargetId\")\nlet isDigit = startsWith(text: \"\\{{targetId}}\", prefix: \"btn-digit\")\nlet routeDigit = if isDigit {\n  // \\{{targetId}} is accessible here from parent scope\n  let exec = execFile(file: \"my-app/handle-digit\")\n}\n```")
}